---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import PdfSecureIWorkToPdf from '@/components/tools/pdf-convert/PdfSecureIWorkToPdf.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="pages-to-pdf">
  <PdfSecureIWorkToPdf client:load kind="pages" />

  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <section>
      <h2>Somebody sent you a .pages file and you are not on a Mac</h2>
      <p>
        Apple's Pages saves in its own format, which no app on Windows or Linux opens. You do not need
        an online account, a new export from the sender, or an upload converter, because the PDF is
        already inside the file. This Pages to PDF tool extracts it.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>How the trick works</h2>
      <p>
        A <code>.pages</code> document is a zip archive. Inside it, alongside Apple's proprietary
        IWA-encoded document data, Pages writes a complete PDF rendering of the document at
        <code>QuickLook/Preview.pdf</code>, so Finder, Quick Look, Spotlight and Mail can show a
        preview without Pages.
      </p>
      <p>
        This page opens the archive in your browser, finds that entry, and gives it to you unchanged.
        There is no rendering step and no font substitution. It is the file Pages made with its real
        layout engine on the machine where the document was written.
      </p>
      <p>
        So it is fast even for a large document, because the tool unzips only one entry. A 200 MB
        document with embedded photographs takes about a second.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>When it will not work, and what to do</h2>
      <div class="overflow-x-auto">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th scope="col">Message</th>
              <th scope="col">Cause</th>
              <th scope="col">Fix</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td>No QuickLook preview inside</td>
              <td>
                Saved with "Include preview in document" turned off, or by an old Pages version
              </td>
              <td>
                Ask the sender to tick that box in Pages → Settings → General and save again, or to
                export a PDF directly
              </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Not a zipped .pages package</td>
              <td>
                The document is a macOS package (a folder that Finder shows as one item), not a
                single file
              </td>
              <td>
                Right-click it on the Mac and choose Compress, then drop the resulting .zip here
              </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>The preview is not a valid PDF</td>
              <td>The archive is truncated, usually from a failed transfer</td>
              <td>Get the file again. Check that the byte count matches</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>What you can and cannot do with the result</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          You can read, print and search it. The preview is a normal PDF with a real text layer, so
          Ctrl-F works and you can copy passages from it.
        </li>
        <li>
          You cannot edit the layout. A PDF is a final-form document. To change the content, the sender
          must export to .docx from Pages, which keeps editable text.
        </li>
        <li>
          You cannot see tracked changes and comments. The preview is the clean rendering of the
          document as it is.
        </li>
        <li>
          You get the exact fonts, because they are embedded in the preview. A conversion that renders
          again on a machine without those fonts cannot do this.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Converting several at once</h2>
      <p>
        Drop as many <code>.pages</code> files as you need. Each one unzips in turn with its own
        progress, and you can save them one by one or all as one zip. The tool lists failed files
        separately with the reason, so one bad document does not stop the batch.
      </p>
      <p>
        The work happens on your machine, so there is no daily limit and no queue. This helps most
        when you receive a folder of somebody's old Pages documents.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>The privacy angle, briefly</h2>
      <p>
        <code>.pages</code> files are often letters, contracts, dissertations and personal
        correspondence, written on someone's own laptop. Here JavaScript in this tab opens the archive,
        and the extracted PDF goes to your downloads folder.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>The other two iWork formats</h2>
      <p>
        Keynote and Numbers use exactly the same package structure, so the same technique works for
        both: <a href="/keynote-to-pdf">Keynote to PDF</a> for <code>.key</code> presentations, and
        <a href="/numbers-to-pdf">Numbers to PDF</a> for <code>.numbers</code> spreadsheets.
      </p>
    </section>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
